From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: UebelAndre <github@uebelandre.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 05:52:29 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(host): ask the client for Bazel data when it is first
 needed

With `initializationOptions.bazel.requests` set, the server starts with
only the workspace and the output base and asks the client for the rest
through three requests: `starpls/bazel/repoMapping` for the mapping of a
canonical repository, `starpls/bazel/buildLanguage` for the native rule
definitions and `starpls/bazel/fetchRepo` to fetch a repository. The
client runs Bazel in the background; nothing in the server blocks, and
startup never waits for Bazel.

The static client records what it was asked for and did not have. A
request whose handler ran into such a gap is held and runs again when the
data arrives, as is a request that found nothing while the main
repository's mapping or the native rules were still on their way (its
miss may be a resolution cached before they came). An answer with
content stands. A held request waits at most once and at most ten
seconds, then answers with what it had; in single-threaded mode the loop
waits for input only until the oldest held request is due, natively
through `poll` and on WASI through `poll_oneoff`, so the bound holds
there as well. Arriving data resets the inference cache (an empty change
does that) and re-analyses the open documents so their diagnostics
follow. Repository fetches go through the client as well, with the usual
progress reporting around them.

Measured on rules_rust with the native binary in single-threaded mode:
the first request that needs the main mapping waits for one
`bazel mod dump_repo_mapping` (about 340 ms); everything after answers
in the usual sub-millisecond to low-millisecond range, and second-hop
mappings inside external repositories are fetched the same way. A click
on a source file in the main repository never waits.
